  "response": "Here's the analysis of the two `singular_plural_type_mismatch` findings in `src/api/state.ts`:\n\n---\n\n**Finding: `crime_00033` — Plural Mismatch**\n\nEvidence (verbatim):\n- `\"plural name, singular type: \\`users: User\\` @L24\"`\n- `\"singular name, array type: \\`invoice: Invoice[]\\` @L27\"`\n- `\"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\"`\n\n---\n\n### 1. `users: User` (line 24)\n\nThe **type carries the intended meaning.** The value is a single object literal `{ id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" }` — clearly one user, not a collection. The plural name `users` is the lie.\n\n**Fix: rename to `user`.**\n\n```ts\nexport const user: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n```\n\nChanging the type to `User[]` would contradict the initializer (a bare object, not an array).\n\n### 2. `invoice: Invoice[]` (line 27)\n\nThe **type carries the intended meaning.** The annotation says `Invoice[]` and the initializer is `[]` — this is a collection. The singular name `invoice` is the lie.\n\n**Fix: rename to `invoices`.**\n\n```ts\nexport const invoices: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\nChanging the type to `Invoice` would contradict the `[]` initializer and require fabricating a default invoice object.\n\n---\n\nBoth cases are name-side fixes. The types and initializers agree with each other; only the names are wrong. Note that lines 33–34 already contain the corrected versions (`user: User` and `invoices: Invoice[]`) as negative controls in this fixture file, confirming the intended shapes.",
